  "description": "<div>\n  <p>Choose the <code>k</code> points in <code>points</code> nearest the origin. Compare squared distance <code>x² + y²</code> so no square roots are needed.</p>\n  <p>For ties, prefer smaller <code>x</code>, then smaller <code>y</code>. Return the chosen points in that same ascending order of (squared distance, x, y), including repeated points as separate entries. Thus the answer has one exact order even when distances tie.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> points = [[1,3],[-2,2]], k = 1\n<strong>Output:</strong> [[-2,2]]</pre>\n  <p>(-2, 2) has squared distance 8 versus 10.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> points = [[3,3],[5,-1],[-2,4]], k = 2\n<strong>Output:</strong> [[3,3],[-2,4]]</pre>\n  <p>(3, 3) and (-2, 4) are the nearest two.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> points = [[1,0],[0,1],[-1,0],[0,-1]], k = 3\n<strong>Output:</strong> [[-1,0],[0,-1],[0,1]]</pre>\n  <p>All four tie; the three lexicographically smallest points win.</p>\n  <p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= points.length &lt;= 1000</code> and <code>0 &lt;= k &lt;= points.length</code>.</li>\n    <li>Every point has exactly two integer coordinates <code>[x, y]</code>, each in <code>[-1000, 1000]</code>.</li>\n    <li>Duplicate points are allowed.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>",
